welcome to another one of my Sadler's  honest book reviews in which I look at  literature dealing with philosophy  ethics leadership self-help and life  generally today I'm going to be talking  about a book that's new but isn't really  new it's how to die an ancient guide to  the end of life which is by Seneca but  edited translated and introduced by  James s ROM and published by Princeton  University Press this is a set of  selections from Seneca that all have to  do with death and I recommend it I think  that anybody who wants to have those  passages that are particularly oriented  towards thinking about the nature of  death and its importance for us as human  beings could benefit from having this  I always like to begin by talking about  3 s's that is the style of the work the  structure and giving a brief summary and  I'm going to do that last one first here  so as I mentioned this is a set of  selections and they're all focused on  the theme of death  so to summarize that this is Seneca  talking about something that he  underwent as a matter of fact it ends  talking about Seneca zone death  unfortunately having to kill himself at  the command of the Emperor Nero and it's  a topic that of course all of us are  going to have to face at one time or  another so that's a good segue then into  talking about the second one which is  the structure of the work and this is a  fairly short book altogether it is just  229 pages but you know small pages and  there are five main sections to the to  the book not including the the materials  at the end each of them is a chapter  consisting of a number of different  passages from Seneca some of them very  short some of them quite long each of  them with a little bit of introductory  material by the editor and translator of  this work so there's there's five  sections the first is prepare yourself  for of course dying the second have no  fear the third have no regrets the  fourth set yourself free the fifth  become part of the whole and then  there's an epilogue practice what you  preach and then you know I mentioned  that the book itself is you know over  200 pages long but the book really only  runs to page 122 because after that it's  Latin texts and the notes which you know  I think some of the readers may be  interested in the notes  very few readers are actually going to  look at the Latin texts but I suppose  it's nice to have them in there there is  a a good introduction at the start by  the editor and entry  later he gives a bio of Seneca if you  haven't you know encounter that already  that's that's useful to have he talks a  little bit about Seneca and stoicism and  does a bit of compare contrast between  Seneca and the other snoke so I think  that material is quite useful as well  when it comes to style we're talking  about translation and so I think it's  probably good to say this is a very  readable translation I think this will  be quite useful for the average person  encountering Seneca you are getting you  know short snippets so you're not  getting full discussions on the part of  Seneca but it's trying to zero in on  some of the main ideas  so what are the main ideas of the work  again this is Seneca so we're getting  Senecas  key ideas with respect to dyeing and  with respect to how we ought to live in  relation to death one of the key themes  is that death is ultimately unavoidable  and you might say well that's a  no-brainer who needs a book to know that  it's something that we often do forget  death and dissolution is inevitable for  all things whether we're talking about  human beings or our surroundings any of  the books we might pick they're all  going to die in their own way so that's  that's one key theme another that's  being treated in the second section is  that death is not the evil that we  typically not just think it to be but  fear it to be and the Stoics spent a lot  of time talking about how to properly  understand death and how to approach it  emotionally so that is another thing  that Seneca is going to talk about  another key theme is that if we think  about death and we do so in a very  realistic way without allowing our  emotions to overtake us without buying  into cultural assumptions that might be  rather questionable but instead putting  them to the test and analyzing them we  can live our lives in a better way so  you could see this as in some respects  not just about how not to feel bad about  death but how to live well you know it's  how to die but the subtitle an ancient  guide to the end of life but it could be  about the rest of our life as well  because we don't know when we're going  to die we don't know what the end of our  life is unless you know we're being  executed or have some sort of disease  that leads to that another key idea that  is particularly interesting given  Senecas own history is that death is an  escape and it's an escape from what  becomes intolerable for a rational be  which could include being bossed around  by a tyrant which is in fact the case  for poor Seneca dealing with Nero he was  an advisor to Nero tried to keep him on  a straight track was very unsuccessful  in doing so there's also a really good  discussion about whether suicide is  legitimate or not the Stoics I won't say  had mixed feelings about this but they  didn't see every single suicide as  legitimate but they did think that that  somebody like Cato who you know is not  that long before Seneca legitimately  committed suicide so there's there's  some some interesting discussion of that  and I'm actually going to read you one  one little passage from the text that  brings a few of these themes together  again this is Senecas own words it's not  death that's glorious but dying  courageously no wooden praises death  rather we praise the person who sold  death stripped away before causing at  any turmoil the death that was glorious  in Cato's case was based and worthy of  shame in Deccan Moses  this is dekha most the man who while  seeking postponement of death though  destined to die drew apart in order to  empty his bowels and when summoned to  his death in order to bear his neck said  I'll bear it if I can live what madness  to take flight when there's no going  backward I'll bear it if I can live he  almost added even under Antony that's a  man worthy to be allowed to live alright  he says so he says as I was discussing  earlier see that death is in itself is  neither good nor bad Cato made the most  honorable use of a dekha most the most  shameful so the idea here is that  suicide and and death in general is what  we make of it and that is another key  idea of the Stoics that Seneca discusses  at great length something else that the  the editor brings out that I think is  quite interesting I hadn't thought about  this beforehand is that Seneca himself  more than the other Stoics really does  talk about death really focuses on it  and you might say well  maybe that's a function of the time that  he lived in you know under these these  despots who are Caesars quite arbitrary  and capricious you know all sorts of  shenanigans going on you know that  there's there's a possibility to that I  don't know that there is less of that  going on in Epictetus world or in Marcus  Aurelius or in you know any of the other  Stoics that we might might run across at  least in part but it is clearly the case  that Seneca did a lot more writing about  it not necessarily more thinking but a  lot more writing than many of the other  stoic so I think that's a great point  that Rahm is is making here  what are the good points about this work  I think there's quite a few and I'm  happy to say what they are the first is  that this is going to be a really good  intro for somebody who hasn't read  Seneca but is interested in stoic  philosophy and I think it's a it's a  great theme it'll help people at least  if they don't agree with Seneca and put  things in perspective in a stoic way  they can at least see what the Stoics  had to say including this major stoic  thinker about the condition that we're  all in and the inevitable death that we  must face so it's a good intro for  Senecas work  it's definitely by somebody who knows  Senecas work inside and out I was you  know very happy to read the translations  I think they really are quite quite  excellent they're gonna be very readable  for the the modern person there's not a  lot of these and thou in there anything  along those lines  it's organised I think in a very useful  way to use five chapters bringing  together these passages quite a good way  to do it he could have touched on more  but then it would have been a longer  book and maybe this is exactly the right  sort of length for the English portion  of the book and the last thing that I'll  say that I really do like about it is  when you're excerpting from an author  it's it's very difficult to decide what  to include and what to leave out and the  tendency is always to try to pack as  much in as possible  rom has selected I think some of the  best passages for conveying Senecas own  views now there's there's more to Seneca  of course than is in here but you can  always go to the other works you can  read the whole letters or read you know  the consolations or read whatever you  want the various treatise by Seneca but  this is good  provide you with an excellent entry  point into thinking about it yeah it's  nice the way that these are gathered  together so that that you can think  primarily about this issue of death  the only negative thing that I do have  to say about this work there's always  some sort of fly in the ointment isn't  there is interestingly something that I  imagine many readers are going to see as  a positive and that's the Latin texts  that are included because if you look at  the book this much of it is latin texts  and so it could have been a much thinner  volume I don't think that adding the  Latin text at the end really does any  good it's not as if scholars are going  to go through this and say oh I'm going  to check his translation I better go to  the the Latin and you know it's not  particularly convenient because it's not  facing pages you got to go back and  forth and the ordinary person are they  going to be reading the Latin I highly  doubt it so you know it just added some  some pages and made the book a bit  thicker so it won't fit so nicely say in  your back pocket but that's a very minor  minor quibble isn't it with with a book  that otherwise is quite good  so to sum up do I recommend this work  definitely and I think this is a great  entry point for anybody who hasn't read  Seneca it's going to be like I mentioned  a very readable translation and  collection of his stuff you're not going  to find anything in here that's not in  other texts that that are available but  it's bringing together these discussions  about death so if you're interested in  that particular topic  I think this book is for you  you

